Argument for “Insufficient Data – Abilities Unknown” 1. Background Feud and Stakes Remain Unresolved a. <quote>"This here Ring ain't ever going to be  the same again. If we don't watch out, we'll find ourselves being  polite </quote> (III). Moe’s warning betrays how alien “politeness” is to ring-rats. The radio announcer confirms the game is simply “the final battle in a feud that has been going on in the Ring for years.” High stakes and deep-seated rivalry favor a decisive outcome, not a mutual ceasefire. 2. Piloting Skill and Ship Reliability Uncertain a. Gus’s teams consist of “rickety spacecraft,” “broomsticks… used to second-rate fuel” that “would split wide open” under the “high test stuff” needed for polo (II). Meek himself needed dead-reckoning and luck to land safely and his stilts almost failed. Even as coach he cannot guarantee all ships survive a single chukker, much less an entire match. Outcome depends critically on technical reliability and piloting – factors not quantified. 3. Meek’s Novice Status Undermines Truce Hypothesis a. Meek “seldom amounts to much” (II) as pilot or coach. He has “never… left Earth” until recently and learned by reading alone. Now he must coach a game he has “never played,” in near-zero gravity on defective craft, with only diagrams and personal enthusiasm. His own near-disaster among the bugs exposes inexperience and lack of authoritative knowledge of space-polo demands. 4. Unknown Mathematical Aptitude of Opponents a. The mysterious “bugs” solve mathematical problems and may indicate an advanced, inscrutable strategy – even sabotage potential. If human competitors mimic their adaptability, the match could devolve into chaos or technical races. No data on human ability to adapt patterns or counter‐strategies midgame. 5. Absence of Precedent and Data a. “I saw a game once,” Meek says, yet that single observation did not yield understanding of “rules and conditions” (III). No two boards were alike. If neither Hamilton nor Meek can decipher bug “games,” they cannot predict human contest. Betting interest (”a fortune bet on his ability
) underscores high uncertainty, not confidence in a truce. Attack on “Truce” Position 1. “Politeness” Contradicted by Radio Broadcast Toward War a. Radio: “the feud… will be played out to its bitter end… decides, forever, the supremacy” (radio). A truce would render the broadcast meaningless; the government’s gamble would collapse. 2. Historical Preference for Feuds and Violence a. “Lots of ships” arrive punctured; “Not many… get killed… willing to take a chance” (I). Ring-rats choose violence over boredom. Introduction of polo may channel anger into a more deadly sport, not reconciliation. 3. Betting and Gambling Incentives a. <quote>”a fortune bet on his ability
</quote> suggests gamblers expect non-truce result; no one would wager sensibly on mutual concession. 4. Lack of Authority to Enforce Truce a. Miss Perkins has no enforcement mechanism. Gus is “nuts about sports” but retains “revenge” motive for stolen injector and “a heap of satisfaction” (II). Nothing compels him not to ram or shoot opponents under guise of “game.” Conclusion Without quantifiable data on piloting expertise, ship integrity, and strategic adaptability—and given the depth of the feud, wartime framing, and high-stakes betting—the likely outcome of the space-polo match is indeterminate. We simply do “not know enough about their abilities to say.”
